In article <22332@ucdavis.ucdavis.edu> zerkle@cs.ucdavis.edu (Dan Zerkle) writes:
>The possibility of instant death is much greater in 3.1.

I agree wholeheartedly.  It's easier to get somewhere, but harder to win.

>Well, some of this could be interpreted as spoilers, but it's mostly
>pretty mild.

>I turned a corner in the Gnomish mines, and saw a Gnome lord.  As I
>saw him (before I could back up or anything), he zapped a wand of
>sleep.  Even though I had about 70 HP and -5 AC at the time, he calmly
>walked up and hit me a few dozen times until I died.  No warning.
>Nothing I can do.  Ouch.

I agree, this is a horrible death--one I've died once or twice--but sleep
resistance isn't vastly hard to come by, because elves are fairly common
and come in packs.  And besides, you could do this to them, why shouldn't
they do it to you?  It does fall in the "instant death" category, though.

>Since the monsters are using the wands, they are all used up by the
>time I get to them.  Few things are more useless than a wand of fire
>with no charges in it.  Ouch.

I think watching a gnome drink a potion of gain level is the worst, though.

>In the future, I expect moving while heavily encumbered to work like
>eating or spellbook reading.  You can stop moving if you want, and 
>start dropping things.

I agree.  As someone else stated, if you stop, you have to begin again from
scratch when you head in that direction, for reasons of code elegance.

>In the future, I expect shopkeepers to welcome all customers,
>invisible or not.  They can detect invisible.  They should just be
>able to sell to anyone.

If shopkeepers can see invisible, and they must, then I see no reason why
they should not welcome the patronage of invisible characters.

>It would seem that magical resistance is essential to this game.  So
>is reflection (shield or amulet).  Unfortunately, I have NEVER managed
>to get magic resistance (outside of being a wizard).  I have only ONCE
>managed to get reflection (that game had two shield and one amulet).
>I have also gotten only one wish.  Given that this equipment is so
>essential, there should perhaps be a side quest to gain these.

A side quest?  Seems kind of drastic.  MR was practically necessary in 3.0,
and everyone just used GDSM (which isn't that hard to get if you're going
to win).  Reflection is indeed a virtual necessity now.  Perhaps reflection
needs to be available through eating some monster or another.


Now on to my personal gripes:

Monsters have much more knowledge than they should.  For example, there was a
level in the Dungeon of Doom with a polymorph trap on it.  I didn't think it
too horrible, especially since I had no cat... at that point, anyway.  However,
every monster that enters this room IMMEDIATELY dives for the polymorph trap.
In the last case, the elf could not have seen anyone else enter the trap, and
I was the only creature in the room capable of speech.  He polymorphed into
a demi-lich, and my game was over.

Furthermore, the monsters have total knowledge of every wand, scroll and potion
that comes into their possession.  If I drop a wand of fire with 1 charge,
that charge will be fired at me.  However, if I drop a wand of speed monster
with 1 charge, they will zap themselves with it.  How could they know?  This
bothers me no end.

Regardless, I will continue to play this game.  And love doing it.

